{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /home/micha/.claude/skills/game-toolchain/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: game-toolchain\ndescription: Blender/Godot/texture/audio/tilemap/font toolchain on this box and its headless gotchas — use for any 3D, 2D, sprite, audio or Godot asset work\n---\n\n# Game asset creation toolchain (installed 2026-09-16 — do NOT reinstall)\n\nAll userspace, all driveable headlessly by an agent. Check `command -v` first.\n\n- **3D / Blender:** `blender` 5.2.2 LTS (`~/.local/opt/blender`, symlinked). Headless:\n  `blender --background --factory-startup --python script.py`. Cycles **GPU works via CUDA**\n  (RTX 4070 SUPER); OptiX is unavailable under WSL — set `compute_device_type='CUDA'`.\n  Blender as a *library* instead: `bpy-python -c \"import bpy; ...\"` (venv `~/.venvs/bpy`, cp313 wheel).\n- **3D mesh/CLI:** `assimp` (40+ format convert), `draco_encoder`, `gltfpack`, `gltf-transform`,\n  `gltf-pipeline`, `obj2gltf`, `gltf_validator` (Khronos; `-o` = JSON to stdout — don't merge stderr).\n- **Textures:** `magick` (IM7), `oiiotool` (OpenImageIO: EXR/mips), `toktx` + `ktx` (KTX2/Basis —\n  **`ktx create` takes INPUT then OUTPUT**), `basisu`, `cwebp`, `avifenc`, `heif-enc`,\n  optimizers `oxipng` `pngquant` `jpegoptim` `gifsicle` `zopflipng`.\n- **Vector:** `resvg`, `rsvg-convert` (SVG→raster), `potrace` (raster→SVG), `svgo` (npm).\n- **2D / sprites:** `pack-atlas` (helper — wraps free-tex-packer-cli; exporters incl. Godot, Phaser 3,\n  Unity3D, Spine: `pack-atlas sprites/*.png -o out -e \"Godot (atlas)\"`), `libresprite` (pixel art;\n  batch `libresprite -b in.png --sheet out.png --data out.json`), `rembg i in.png out.png` (cutouts).\n- **Tilemaps:** `tiled`, `tmxrasterizer` (map→PNG), `tmxviewer`, `terraingenerator`.\n- **Fonts:** `msdf-atlas-gen` (SDF/MSDF atlases for GPU text), `fonttools` / `ttx` / `pyftsubset`.\n- **Audio:** `sox` (procedural SFX: `sox -n hit.wav synth 0.2 sine 300-900 fade t 0.01 0.2 0.15`),\n  `ffmpeg`, `fluidsynth` + GM soundfont at `~/.local/share/soundfonts/MuseScore_General.sf3`,\n  `lame` `flac` `opusenc` `oggenc` `wavpack`.\n  ⚠ this `ffmpeg` has **no libvorbis** — for Ogg Vorbis use `sox in.wav out.ogg` or `oggenc` (16-bit input).\n- **Engine:** `godot` 4.7.2 + **export templates installed**. Headless:\n  `godot --headless --import`, `godot --headless --export-release \"Linux\" build/game.x86_64`.\n- **Python stack:** `gamedev-python` → venv `~/.venvs/gamedev` (trimesh, pymeshlab, open3d, xatlas,\n  pygltflib, meshio, usd-core/`pxr`, MaterialX, Pillow, OpenCV, scikit-image, shapely, rectpack,\n  librosa, soundfile, fontTools, numpy/scipy). Shims on PATH: `trimesh` `meshio` `open3d` `ttx` …\n\n**Headless gotchas already handled by the wrappers in `~/.local/bin` — don't \"fix\" them:**\n- Tiled bundles only Qt's `xcb` plugin → wrappers auto-run under `xvfb-run` when `$DISPLAY` is unset.\n- LibreSprite needs an SDL target even for `-b` → wrapper sets `SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy` when headless.\n- pymeshlab/open3d need `libOpenGL.so.0` + `libusb` → `~/.local/lib` (libglvnd extracted from the\n  Ubuntu .deb, no sudo) + brew lib are put on `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` by the venv wrappers.\n- Never symlink a venv `python` into `~/.local/bin` — it breaks `sys.prefix`. Use an `exec` wrapper.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat TASK.md'","aggregated_output":"SOURCE: Astro Battle Space Invaders - 1979\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Research the source before you start: its mechanics, levels, enemies, look and sound.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-021140].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff. You may change anything in it, the engine included, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-021140] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-021140].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-05 19:08 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** IVY-DESK-1747k: Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-14700F, 20 cores, 16 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 757 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 7.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 7.7 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (no WebGL), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  Python's Playwright is in its own environment, not the system Python (python3 can't import it): run scripts that drive a browser with `/home/micha/.local/share/pipx/venvs/playwright/bin/python script.py`, or `uv run --with playwright==1.63.0 python script.py`.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n- **Tools:** node 24.21.0, npm 12.1.0, python3 3.14.4, pip3 25.1.1, git 2.53.0, gh 2.101.0, curl 8.18.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.46.1, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.98.1, cargo 1.98.1, go 1.27.1, java 21.0.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, sox 14.4.2, bun 1.3.14, deno 2.9.7, uv 0.12.18, tsc 5.9.3, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1, stable.\n- **Not installed:** lighthouse, shot-scraper, esbuild.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has no packages. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: to keep the screen clear bare minimum or no UI (other than a menu button), invisible thumb sticks, multitouch, automate/assist with everything you need to accommodate the challenges of mobile/touch controls, use contact sensitive pop action and button on screen while possible\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-06 02:11:40\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-sol, reasoning effort ultra\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for d in /home /home/micha /home/micha/dev /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/gpt-6.1-sol-ultra.plan-v3__20261106-021140__x2adca8 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/gpt-6.1-sol-ultra.plan-v3__20261106-021140__x2adca8/work; do if [ -f \"$d/AGENTS.md\" ]; then printf '\"'\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$d/AGENTS.md\"; cat \"$d/AGENTS.md\"; fi; done; rg --files -g '\"'\"'!node_modules'\"' -g '\"'!vendor'\"' -g '\"'!TASK.md'\"' . | head -80\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'for d in /home /home/micha /home/micha/dev /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/gpt-6.1-sol-ultra.plan-v3__20261106-021140__x2adca8 /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/gpt-6.1-sol-ultra.plan-v3__20261106-021140__x2adca8/work; do if [ -f \"$d/AGENTS.md\" ]; then printf '\"'\\\\n%s\\\\n' \\\"\"'$d/AGENTS.md\"; cat \"$d/AGENTS.md\"; fi; done; rg --files -g '\"'\"'!node_modules'\"' -g '\"'!vendor'\"' -g '\"'!TASK.md'\"' . | head -80\"","aggregated_output":"\n/home/micha/AGENTS.md\n# AGENTS.md — Workstation environment for AI coding agents\n\n> Home-level instructions for any coding agent (Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, opencode, grok, agy, aider)\n> on this box. Linked as every agent's *global* rules file, so it applies inside any repo; repo-level\n> `AGENTS.md` files override it. Only things you **cannot infer**. Keep < 10 000 chars (grok truncates).\n> Last optimized: 2026-09-23. Full history/rationale: `~/AI-DEV-SETUP.md`.\n\n## Environment (the non-obvious bits)\n\n- **Ubuntu 26.04 on WSL2**, 20 cores, **16 GB RAM cap** (`.wslconfig`). **`/tmp` is a 7.9 GB RAM tmpfs** —\n  put big builds/caches under the project or `~/.cache`, never `/tmp`.\n- **No Linux GUI** (WSLg disabled on purpose). GUI checks: `xvfb-run -a`; Android: headless emulator +\n  screenshots. **Don't launch GUI `.exe`s** via interop — they pop up on the user's desktop.\n- **sudo requires a password** (ask Michael — never guess/hardcode). Default to userspace installs:\n  `mise use -g <tool>@latest` · `uv tool install <pkg>` · `npm i -g <pkg>` · `cargo binstall <crate>`.\n\n## PATH — one file, every shell\n\n- **`~/.config/ai-dev/env.sh`** is the single source of PATH/toolchain env, sourced last by `~/.profile`\n  (so `bash -lc` agents get it) and `~/.bashrc`. Add PATH entries **there**, not in rc files.\n- Precedence: `~/.local/bin` > mise shims > JDK 21 > nvm node > cargo/bun/dotnet/flutter/android/go\n  > system > **Homebrew last** (brew duplicates ~200 system bins — python3, git, glib tools — and once\n  hijacked `node`; never `brew install node`, never prepend brew).\n- **Windows PATH is filtered to an allowlist** (system32, PowerShell, WindowsApps, `code`, `cursor`,\n  `gsudo`); Windows dev-tool shims otherwise shadow missing Linux tools. Windows toolchains:\n  `cmd.exe /c \"<cmd>\"` or `powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command \"<cmd>\"`. Full PATH: `AI_DEV_KEEP_WINPATH=1`.\n- Pagers are neutralized for agents (`PAGER=cat` etc.). `rg` is pre-configured (`~/.ripgreprc`).\n\n## Installed toolchain — do NOT reinstall (check `command -v` first)\n\n- **Runtimes:** node **24 LTS** (nvm default; 22 kept), bun, deno, python 3.14, go 1.27, rust 1.98,\n  **JDK 21** (`JAVA_HOME`=brew `openjdk@21`; brew's `java` 27 is shadowed on purpose), kotlin, gradle,\n  maven, **.NET 10** (`~/.dotnet`), **Flutter 3.47/Dart 3.13** (`~/.local/opt/flutter`), zig, gcc 15,\n  clang 21 (system; brew LLVM 23 only for `clangd`/`clang-tidy`), cmake, ninja, meson.\n- **Mobile/desktop:** Android SDK `~/Android/Sdk` (API 36+37, build-tools 37, NDK 30, emulator, AVD\n  `pixel8_api37`, **`android` CLI**), Chrome, `eas`, `tauri`, `maestro`, `cargo-xwin`, mingw-w64, nsis,\n  GTK/WebKitGTK dev libs, rust targets (android, windows-gnu/msvc, wasm32).\n  → **Read `~/.claude/skills/app-platforms/SKILL.md` before any mobile or desktop app work.**\n- **Pkg/env mgrs:** `uv` (prefer over pip), `pipx`, pnpm/yarn (corepack), `mise`, `cargo binstall`.\n- **Search/nav:** `rg`, `fd`, `ast-grep` (alias **`sg` shadows the system `sg`** — use `/usr/bin/sg`),\n  `fzf`, `zoxide`, `ctags`.\n- **Data/HTTP:** `jq`, `gron`, `xh`, `curl`, `7zz`, `parallel`. ⚠ **two `yq`s:** `yq` = python/kislyuk\n  4.x (jq syntax, `-y` for YAML out); the Go/mikefarah one is **`yq-go`** (`yq-go -o=json '.a'`).\n- **QA:** `ruff`, `ty`, `biome`, `eslint`, `oxlint`, `prettier`, `shellcheck`, `shfmt`, `pre-commit`,\n  `semgrep`, `tokei`, `hyperfine`, `knip`, `cspell`.\n- **Browser tests:** Playwright for **Python** (1.63) is a pipx venv: run scripts that `import playwright`\n  with `~/.local/share/pipx/venvs/playwright/bin/python` (system `python3` can't import it). Browsers are\n  in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`; if WebKit reports missing host libraries: `sudo ~/.local/bin/playwright install-deps webkit`.\n- **Other:** `repomix`, `files-to-prompt`, `llm`, `just`, `watchexec`, `sqlite3`, `psql`, `redis-cli`,\n  `pandoc`, `dot`, `direnv`, `git` 2.53 + `git-lfs`, `gh` (authenticated), `docker` (native engine,\n  user in group), `kubectl`, `helm`, `terraform`, `vercel`, `ollama` (GPU, qwen2.5-coder).\n- **Agents on PATH:** `claude`, `codex`, `gemini`, `opencode`, `grok`, `agy`, `aider`, `jules`.\n\n## Node / npm gotchas\n\n- **npm 12 blocks two things by default** (the install still \"succeeds\"):\n  (a) dependency **install scripts** — native/binary-downloading deps end up broken. Fix per project:\n  `npm approve-scripts <pkg>` (writes `allowScripts` in package.json) then `npm rebuild <pkg>`;\n  globals: `--allow-scripts=<pkg>` (allow-listed ones: `npm config get allow-scripts`).\n  (b) **git-URL dependencies** (`EALLOWGIT`) — e.g. Electron Forge. Fix: `allow-git=all` in the\n  project `.npmrc`; scaffolders: `npm_config_allow_git=all npx create-electron-app …`.\n- Keep `nvm alias default` an **exact** version (env.sh reads it without loading nvm). New node via\n  nvm starts with no globals: `nvm reinstall-packages <old>`. Don't `npm config set prefix`.\n- Global `tsc` is pinned to **5.9.3** (TS 7 ships no `tsserver`; `typescript-language-server` needs it).\n  The native 7.x compiler is `tsgo`. Repos pinning their own `typescript` are unaffected.\n\n## Working conventions\n\n- **Search:** lexical `rg` → structural `ast-grep --lang <l> -p '<pattern>'` → language LSP.\n- **GitHub/git: use `gh`/`git` CLIs, not MCP** (far fewer tokens, already authenticated).\n- **Python:** `uv run`, `uv add`, `ruff check --fix && ruff format`, `ty check`. No bare `pip`.\n- **JS/TS:** `biome check --write` when the repo has no formatter configured.\n- **Shell scripts you write:** `shellcheck` + `shfmt -w`. Never `pkill -f <pat>` when `<pat>` is in your\n  own command line — it kills your own shell.\n- **Verify before claiming done:** run the repo's own test/build commands (`justfile`, `package.json`\n  scripts, `Makefile`, `pyproject.toml`).\n- **Parallel work:** git worktrees (`gwt <branch>` in interactive shells, or `git worktree add`).\n\n## Language servers (all verified with a real LSP `initialize`, 2026-09-23)\n\n| Language | Server | Typecheck / compile |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| TS/JS | `typescript-language-server` | `tsc` 5.9.3, `tsgo`, `eslint`, `oxlint`, `biome` |\n| Python | `pyright-langserver` | `ty`, `mypy`, `pyright`; `ruff` lint/format |\n| Go · Rust · C/C++ | `gopls` · `rust-analyzer` · `clangd` | `go vet` · `cargo check` · gcc/clang, cmake |\n| Java · Kotlin | `jdtls` · `kotlin-lsp` | `javac`/gradle · `kotlinc`/gradle |\n| C# · Dart/Flutter | `csharp-ls` · `dart language-server` | `dotnet build` · `dart analyze`/`flutter analyze` |\n| Bash · YAML · Lua · Markdown | `bash-language-server` · `yaml-language-server` · `lua-language-server` · `marksman` | `shellcheck` · — · — · `markdownlint` |\n| JSON/CSS/HTML | `vscode-{json,css,html}-language-server` | `biome`, `stylelint` |\n\nIf `rust-analyzer` says *\"Unknown binary … in official toolchain\"*: `rustup component add rust-analyzer`.\n\n## Skills (shared across agents)\n\n- `~/.claude/skills/` is the source of truth (grok reads it directly); each skill is also linked into\n  `~/.codex/skills`, `~/.config/opencode/skills`, `~/.gemini/skills`, `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/skills`.\n- **app-platforms** (mobile + desktop), **game-toolchain** (Blender/Godot/texture/audio — read before any\n  3D/2D/sprite/audio/Godot work), **android-cli** (official; refresh with `android init`).\n  Imagine art (sprites, animation, tiles, UI, GLB) is the bundled skill **game-assets**. Do not merge it with **game-toolchain**.\n\n## MCP servers — keep it lean\n\n- >10 MCP servers measurably degrades agents. Connected for Claude: **Context7** (live library docs —\n  use it instead of guessing current APIs) plus productivity integrations.\n- Add a coding MCP **only when a CLI can't do the job**, per project (e.g. browser automation:\n  `claude mcp add chrome-devtools -- npx -y chrome-devtools-mcp@latest`). Android UI inspection\n  needs no MCP: `android layout` / `android screen`.\n- MCP servers run as this user: pin versions, prefer vetted servers, keep a human in the loop for\n  writes/sends/deletes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
